## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKBDT1NbUAEmRB1?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096/contextkingceo/article/2062725510715822266/media/2062722637315657729) Selection, Not Capacity: The Case for Context Infrastructure...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Nishkarsh</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/contextkingceo">@contextkingceo</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096">http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096">http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HKBDT1NbUAEmRB1?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096/contextkingceo/article/2062725510715822266/media/2062722637315657729)<br/>Selection, Not Capacity: The Case for Context Infrastructure <br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1904944251517157376/gBD-1YKd_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096/contextkingceo)<br/>[Nishkarsh](http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096/contextkingceo)<br/>[@contextkingceo](http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096/contextkingceo)<br/>·<br/>14h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow contextkingceo<br/>1<br/>3<br/>37<br/>[1.7K](http://x.com/i/article/2062719542636548096/contextkingceo/status/2062725510715822266/analytics)<br/>Bigger context windows and bolt-on memory don't fix long-horizon agents, because the bottleneck was never how much you can store. It's deciding what the model attends to and that's the layer that is worth building as infrastructure. Every team building autonomous agents eventually meets the same wall. A capable model, given tools and a long task, performs beautifully for twenty steps, then drifts, contradicts itself, and falls apart somewhere around step eighty. The reflex is to reach for more: a model with a longer context window, or a memory system that lets the agent store what it learns and recall it later. Both are reasonable. Neither works the way people expect. Understanding the why points directly at the most valuable, least understood layer in the agent stack.<br/>## <br/>The failure is a loop.<br/>The reason agents degrade isn't a shortage of capability. It's a feedback loop, and it has four links.<br/>It starts with a fact that undermines everything built on top of it: a model's ability to use its context is not uniform across that context, and it gets worse as the context fills. Models reliably use information at the start and end of their context and systematically under-attend to the middle, even when they're built for long inputs. Output reliability falls as input grows even on tasks as trivial as repeating back a list of words, with a single distractor measurably hurting performance and several compounding. <br/>The effective context, which is the part a model can actually reason over reliably, is far smaller than the advertised window and it shrinks as you pack more in. So an agent that accumulates history and tool output as it works is steadily lowering the quality of every step it takes. The growing context manufactures per-step errors.<br/>Per-step error would be tolerable if agents took a few steps. They take many, and the failures multiply rather than add. An agent that is 95% reliable on each step becomes closer to coin flips as the steps increase. This is why long-horizon autonomy has lagged so far behind raw capability. The drifts are also self-reinforcing in nature. One tool call that goes off-trajectory makes the next one significantly more likely. Stack that on the first link and the base error rate is itself climbing as the window fills. That's why agents don't degrade gracefully. They hold, then they unpredictably cliff. <br/>A long task therefore has to put state somewhere outside the model, because language models are stateless. Every call begins blank and retains nothing except what you feed back. The answer so far has been to externalize: scratchpads and progress files, checkpoints, vector stores, dedicated memory layers. Some borrow the operating-system metaphor directly, paging information between the context window and larger external stores; memory products extract facts and re-serve them across sessions. This is correct and necessary, and it looks like a clean resolution. The agent forgets nothing important because everything important lives in durable storage.<br/>Except stored memory is inert. The model cannot reason over a database; it can only reason over what is</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 43 · 🔁 3 · 🔖 35</p><p><a href="https://x.com/contextkingceo/status/2062725510715822266">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
