# New Aesthetics Grants * [Andy Allen](https://notbor.ing/) Andy is highlighting the aesthetics and cultural impact of software. * Luke Burgis * [Spencer Chang](https://spencer.place) Spencer Chang is an artist and engineer making software you can touch and will create a participatory artwork that preserves and activates our traces across the Internet. * [Adam Davies and Jack Marsh](https://www.notquitepast.com/about/) Adam Davies and Jack Marsh (Not Quite Past) work on what they call the...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Patrick Collison</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/patrickc">@patrickc</a>)</p><blockquote><p>Tyler and I just published a list of the recipients of the New Aesthetics grants: <a href="https://newaesthetics.art/grants">https://newaesthetics.art/grants</a>.<br/><br/>Thank you very much to all who applied. There were far more applications than we expected. We funded 28 grantees and are excited to see what they create.<br/><br/>My reflections on the whole thing:<br/><br/>• Though there are clearly selection dynamics afoot, figuring out some route beyond the current aesthetic moment seems to be of wider interest in the art community than I would have guessed. Many applicants described their dissatisfaction with the status quo, some in strong terms. We had to close applications after a few weeks because there were so many.<br/>• It's too early to call it, but it seems that both beauty as an unapologetic goal (contra much that is in modernist and contemporary approaches), and ways to channel pre-modern styles into something new for the present era, are of growing interest.<br/>• The awards made me reflect on the perhaps obvious issue of how hard it must be for an artist to persistently do something new: schools, galleries, buyers, etc., all have structurally embedded preferences as well. These individual awards made me wonder what form supporting new clusters could take.<br/>• Architecture seems to me like the discipline most ripe for new ideas. One correspondent observed: "American architects are somewhat constrained by the association with the academy, in addition to the well known regulation issues. There is a tendency to overthink things so that the designs are formally interesting to someone deep in the conversation, but lacking poetry and magic. There are more firms in Europe, South America and beyond that “just do things” (especially in places where it is easier to build)." This was evident in the submissions.<br/>• AI seems to be making people rethink things in a quite fundamental way, just as urbanization/industrialization/popularization of photography did at the end of the 19th century. For some that will mean interesting new forms of AI-augmented art, but the effects of the rethinking will likely be wider.<br/>• Arts funding is clearly as precarious and scarce as ever. That's unfortunate, but it probably also means that individual actors can have meaningful impact, and I encourage others to get involved if interested.<br/>• There's a lot to know that is not written down, and I'm very grateful to those who have helped and advised me along the way.</p></blockquote><div><img src="https://pub-bb2e103a32db4e198524a2e9ed8f35b4.r2.dev/e6982fae-41a0-4ed6-a439-d6de49f22dee/id-preview-ecf22203--8effec78-7664-4b2c-9a66-de1ab6241865.lovable.app-1780168940544.png" alt="" style="max-width:480px;margin:4px"/></div><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="https://newaesthetics.art/grants">A Call for New Aesthetics</a></p><blockquote># New Aesthetics Grants<br/>* [Andy Allen](https://notbor.ing/)  <br/>Andy is highlighting the aesthetics and cultural impact of software.<br/>* Luke Burgis<br/>* [Spencer Chang](https://spencer.place)  <br/>Spencer Chang is an artist and engineer making software you can touch and will create a participatory artwork that preserves and activates our traces across the Internet.<br/>* [Adam Davies and Jack Marsh](https://www.notquitepast.com/about/)  <br/>Adam Davies and Jack Marsh (Not Quite Past) work on what they call the future's recovery of the past, starting with AI-designed Delft tiles.<br/>* [Alihan Dumankaya](https://estudio.ad/)  <br/>Alihan Dumankaya is an architect based in Barcelona who explores architectural languages and ways of creating places with more life.<br/>* [Megan Gafford](https://www.fashionablylatetakes.com/p/towards-a-new-aesthetic)  <br/>I’m going to try to invent a new ornamental style of architecture through drawing, because just as writing is thinking, drawing is imagining.<br/>* [Mark Gorenstein](https://oeuvre.art/)  <br/>œuvre editions aims to marry the traditional crafts of printing and bookmaking with modern technological and aesthetic developments.<br/>* [Ruben Hanssen](https://theaestheticcity.com/towards-a-new-aesthetics/)  <br/>A short documentary series on the YouTube channel The Aesthetic City, exploring the future of aesthetics, connecting time-tested principles with the latest technologies.<br/>* [Matthew Henderson](https://x.com/instance%5F11)  <br/>My work explores how designing solely with code can take full advantage of digital fabrication and thereby enable a world where all precious things are unique.<br/>* [Chelsea Hodson](https://www.rosebooks.co/)  <br/>Rose Books is an independent publisher in Sedona, Arizona, which will use the New Aesthetics grant to support the production and operations costs for publication of the next two titles.<br/>* Rui Huang<br/>* [Zahra Jewanjee](https://www.zjewanjee.com)  <br/>Zahra Jewanjee's multidisciplinary practice oscillates between painting, photography, sculpture, and found materials, echoing interconnectedness and interdependence within nature.<br/>* [Joseph Jutras](https://www.institute-of-traditional-architecture.org/)  <br/>Jutras is an advocate for beauty in our built environment and will use the grant towards founding a full-time classical architecture academy.<br/>* [Ben Lee](https://www.linkedin.com/in/benlee01)  <br/>My work leverages emerging 21st-century tools to move beyond default building practices, aiming to cultivate a vibrant architectural language that defines the visual character and aesthetic ambition of our generation.<br/>* [Bhumika Mukherjee](https://bhumikamukherjee.com/)  <br/>My work tries to depict the projected abstraction of life as perceived by life.<br/>* [Karyn Nakamura](https://karynnakamura.com)  <br/>Building a studio that translates between technology and culture through creative direction, experimental R&amp;D, and technical production.<br/>* [Not Nothing](https://www.notnothing.ooo/)  <br/>Not Nothing publishes unfashionable work from hermits and informants, for human and non-human readers, while experimenting with how books are constructed so their discourse may outlast them.<br/>* [Sam Peitz](https://x.com/samdape)  <br/>We use AI in ways that flatten us to a common denominator. Experimenting with ones that do the opposite, stripping design back to its fundamentals.<br/>* [Hamzah Randhawa](https://x.com/haramzah2/status/2058709416547901688?s=20)  <br/>Creating a workshop/lab to prototype both temporary and permanent modular 3D-printed and CNC milled bespoke ceiling facades inspired by various cultural heritages.<br/>* [Michiel Rauws](https://michielrauws.me/sf-undergrounding)  <br/>Power cables make San Francisco streets look like we live in the year 1900, but not for much longer.<br/>* [San Modern](https://sanmodern.com)  <br/>The grant will support our development of a silent, analog meditation timer—a first step toward our mission of bringing more care, wonder and aesthetic conviction back into everyday objects.<br/>* [Kristina Saint](https://www.kvsaint.com/)</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 1,049 · 🔁 121 · 💬 88 · 🔖 767</p><p><a href="https://x.com/patrickc/status/2058931538029662435">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
      <media:content url="https://pub-bb2e103a32db4e198524a2e9ed8f35b4.r2.dev/e6982fae-41a0-4ed6-a439-d6de49f22dee/id-preview-ecf22203--8effec78-7664-4b2c-9a66-de1ab6241865.lovable.app-1780168940544.png" medium="image" />
