## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ_pV0ibsAAPVEe?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240/itsalexvacca/article/2062623990192251082/media/2062623715448565760) The 5-Channel Funnel That Added $307K MRR for us in a Single Quarter...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Alex Vacca</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/itsalexvacca">@itsalexvacca</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240">http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240">http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ_pV0ibsAAPVEe?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240/itsalexvacca/article/2062623990192251082/media/2062623715448565760)<br/>The 5-Channel Funnel That Added $307K MRR for us in a Single Quarter<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1909705035052990467/pnfX3LA__normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240/itsalexvacca)<br/>[Alex Vacca](http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240/itsalexvacca)<br/>[@itsalexvacca](http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240/itsalexvacca)<br/>·<br/>18h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow itsalexvacca<br/>1<br/>7<br/>26<br/>[12K](http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240/itsalexvacca/status/2062623990192251082/analytics)<br/>Most founders try to fix the wrong end of the funnel.<br/>Reply rates dip, so they rewrite subject lines. Close rates soften, so they rebuild the pitch deck. Months disappear into optimizing the bottom of a system that's actually starving at the top. The constraint was never the copy. The machine just wasn't taking in enough qualified people to begin with.<br/>We spent Q1 proving the opposite case. 100,000 unique visitors to the site. 200+ meetings booked. $307K in new MRR added, on a business already past $7M ARR and built without a dollar of outside money. None of that came from a cleverer email. It came from treating lead generation as the lever everything else hangs off, then building the rest of the funnel so almost nothing we generated leaked back out.<br/>There's a lazy version of this idea going around, the one that says a strong top of funnel earns you the right to a sloppy bottom. That reading is wrong, and acting on it gets expensive fast. Pour 100,000 visitors into a quarter and a 5% leak stops being a rounding error. It's thousands of in-market buyers walking out with your acquisition cost in their pocket. At that scale a weak funnel doesn't just underperform. It bleeds money in direct proportion to how much traffic you feed it.<br/>So we built both ends on purpose. Here's the whole system, stage by stage, with the actual stack behind it.<br/># <br/>Why volume changes the entire problem<br/>A funnel that handles 2,000 visitors a quarter and one that handles 100,000 are not the same machine at two sizes. They break in different places.<br/>At low volume, your problem is genuine scarcity. You don't have enough at-bats, so every conversation has to convert, and the work lives downstream: sharper targeting, better calls, tighter follow-up. Most GTM advice is written for this world, which is why it fixates on messaging.<br/>At high volume, scarcity becomes a routing problem. The leads already exist. The real question is whether your system can catch, score, and route them before they cool off. The bottleneck shifts from "can we get a meeting" to "can we hold the 94 people who raised a hand but weren't ready to book today." Lose them and you've built an expensive way to hand buyers to your competitors.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ-EgbuaUAAr-Sp?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2062512037541130240/itsalexvacca/article/2062623990192251082/media/2062512847092076544)<br/>Leads lost to a 5% capture leak, by quarterly traffic<br/>That chart is the whole reason we refuse to treat the bottom of the funnel as optional. A 5% leak that costs 100 leads at low volume costs 5,000 at ours. Founders between $500K and $5M usually keep optimizing for scarcity long after their real problem quietly became volume management. We built the entire funnel around the volume problem, in four stages.<br/># <br/>Stage 1: Five channels into one pipe<br/>Traffic comes from five places, and the number isn't the point.e backend. What matters is that the channels are coordinated and they all empty into the sam<br/>Automated outbound, LinkedIn content, LinkedIn Ads, Google Ads, and SEO. A prospect might catch a team member's LinkedIn p</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 27 · 🔁 6 · 💬 1 · 🔖 124</p><p><a href="https://x.com/itsalexvacca/status/2062623990192251082">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
