## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJU4BCjWMAMGT8H?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484/AdamBartas/article/2062435013484814400/media/2059613995108020227) Figma Is No Longer the Best Place for Product Design...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Adam Barta</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/AdamBartas">@AdamBartas</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484">http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484">http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJU4BCjWMAMGT8H?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484/AdamBartas/article/2062435013484814400/media/2059613995108020227)<br/>Figma Is No Longer the Best Place for Product Design <br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1909312276185669632/IxorQk1j_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484/AdamBartas)<br/>[Adam Barta](http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484/AdamBartas)<br/>[@AdamBartas](http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484/AdamBartas)<br/>·<br/>18h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow AdamBartas<br/>6<br/>5<br/>36<br/>[8.5K](http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484/AdamBartas/status/2062435013484814400/analytics)<br/>Figma is dead for product design.<br/>That sounds dramatic. It is also how the last few weeks have felt for me.<br/>On my latest project, I saved more than 60 hours by not skipping Figma as the main deliverable and using code to create a prototype instead.<br/>Not because Figma is bad. Figma is still one of the best tools ever made for visual design, design systems, component libraries, and collaboration. But for a lot of product design work, especially early prototypes, the current workflow is really slow.<br/>The traditional path looks like this:<br/>* Map the product flow<br/>* Design the main screens<br/>* Build or maintain the design system<br/>* Create every state and variant<br/>* Hand it off<br/>* When feedback comes in, make sure the change is applied across all screens<br/>This flow used to make sense because the design canvas was the fastest place to explore product ideas.<br/>It’s not anymore.<br/># <br/>Ditch the boring part<br/>Instead of spending days designing repetitive screens, designers can now start from product context, and user flows, sketch layouts of the important screens, plug in a component system like shadcn/ui, and let AI generate a working interface.<br/>Not only is this way faster, but the client also gets delivered an interactive prototype that they can click through and in which they can change the mocked-up data. This is simply not possible in Figma.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJU4IqoWYAMRvJa?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484/AdamBartas/article/2062435013484814400/media/2059614126125506563)<br/>That is invaluable for early product decisions.<br/>It also compresses a lot of repetitive work on the designer's side.<br/>You do not need to manually design the tenth settings screen from scratch. You do not need to keep recreating tables, empty states, modals, filters, and form layouts just to prove the same system over and over.<br/>If the product direction is clear, AI can generate a large part of it faster than a designer can.<br/>The designer’s job shifts from drawing every screen to directing the system, tweaking details, and most importantly, testing a lot more flow variations and ideas than they would have been able to otherwise.<br/># <br/>Don't reinvent the wheel<br/>Creating your own design system doesn’t make sense in most cases. Start with a proven component library and build on top of it.<br/>[shadcn/ui](https://ui.shadcn.com/)<br/> works well in this workflow because it gives the AI a strong starting point, but offers heavy customization options<br/>The components already have a good structure. The accessibility basics are better than most hand-made UI kits. The patterns are close to what many modern products need. And with the new advanced themes, you can even add some character to the interface right out of the box.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJU4RP_W0AMskue?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2059613463731732484/AdamBartas/article/2062435013484814400/media/2059614273593069571)<br/>Instead of rebuilding the wheel, designers can just use shadcn as the base.<br/>That means the designer can focus on the things that actually matter:<br/>* How should the flows work?<br/>* What should the user understand on each</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 44 · 🔁 3 · 💬 8 · 🔖 99</p><p><a href="https://x.com/AdamBartas/status/2062435013484814400">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
