## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) Did someone say … cookies? X and its partners use cookies to provide you with a better, safer and faster service and to support our business. Some cookies are necessary to use our services, improve our services, and make sure they work properly. Show more about your choices. Accept all cookies Refuse non-essential cookies...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Vijay Iyengar</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/vijayiyengar">@vijayiyengar</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696">http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696">http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>Did someone say … cookies?<br/>X and its partners use cookies to provide you with a better, safer and faster service and to support our business. Some cookies are necessary to use our services, improve our services, and make sure they work properly. Show more about your choices.<br/>Accept all cookies<br/>Refuse non-essential cookies<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ85U4SawAABewH?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696/vijayiyengar/article/2062429159545303409/media/2062430185228779520)<br/>Customer-facing agents are a different beast<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2047058151942615040/t0bdNbNc_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696/vijayiyengar)<br/>[Vijay Iyengar](http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696/vijayiyengar)<br/>[@vijayiyengar](http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696/vijayiyengar)<br/>·<br/>3h<br/>·<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow vijayiyengar<br/>2<br/>7<br/>[598](http://x.com/i/article/2059705598988701696/vijayiyengar/status/2062429159545303409/analytics)<br/>I sometimes get asked "Why is Sierra building an agent platform? Can't you just use Claude Code or Codex as your agent?"<br/>The answer is that Codex and Claude are fantastic coding agents. But what our customers need, and what Sierra is building, is a platform for building customer-facing agents.<br/>Let's dive in.<br/># <br/>Back-office vs front-office<br/>Most AI agent discourse is focused on the back-office; ie: agents that talk to your employees and perform coding, analytics, finance, and knowledge work tasks. Codex and Claude in particular have taken off for this use case, and are investing heavily here.<br/>But the front-office, ie: agents that talk to your customers, are arguably more important. As Peter Drucker says in <br/>[The Effective Executive](https://www.amazon.com/Effective-Executive-Definitive-Harperbusiness-Essentials-ebook/dp/B01F1WZGNC)<br/>:<br/>&gt; The single most important thing to remember about any enterprise is that results exist only on the outside. The result of a business is a satisfied customer. ...Inside an enterprise there are only costs.<br/>Customer-facing agents are the front-office. They help businesses serve their customers. They can pick up on a 1-800 number at all hours of the day, speak any language, and never put you on hold. They can power search and discovery for a travel booking experience (travel agents are back!). They can handle handle complex, multi-step workflows like filing an insurance claim or applying for a mortgage.<br/>This means a better experience for customers (no being put on hold or re-explaining yourself), better retention, and lower cost for businesses. AI is the obvious enabler of this.<br/># <br/>What makes customer-facing agents hard?<br/>Agents are just <br/>[LLMs calling tools in a loop](https://simonwillison.net/2025/May/22/tools-in-a-loop/)<br/>. Codex and Claude are great agent harnesses. Why not use them to build front-office agents?<br/>Let's unpack what makes customer-facing agents different, and hard.<br/>## <br/>Voice-first, not text-first<br/>Most back-office work happens over text in a computer. This is the modality that Claude and Codex have optimized for (indeed: Anthropic doesn't have a voice model at all yet).<br/>Most customer service happens over the phone, so voice is table stakes. The voice modality brings a number of challenges that coding agents don't have to consider:<br/>1. Turn detection: There's no concept of a discrete turn, so you have to determine when the user is done talking. Does "um" mean there's more or is the user done?<br/>2. Interrupts: Was the user just clearing their throat or did they have more to say? Did them yelling "don't touch that!" to their dog mean the agent should stop what it's doing?<br/>3. Progress: When coding agents take time, they can show you reasoning and tool calls inline. How do you "show" progress conversationally over audio in a realistic w</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 25 · 🔁 6 · 🔖 27</p><p><a href="https://x.com/vijayiyengar/status/2062429159545303409">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
