      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>regent0x</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/regent0x_">@regent0x_</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456">http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456">http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>Did someone say … cookies?<br/>X and its partners use cookies to provide you with a better, safer and faster service and to support our business. Some cookies are necessary to use our services, improve our services, and make sure they work properly. Show more about your choices.<br/>Accept all cookies<br/>Refuse non-essential cookies<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ4HpEEXMAAbDeO?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456/regent0x%5F/article/2062099361199263928/media/2062094081430335488)<br/>How to Run 100 Agents in Parallel in Claude Code (Full Playbook)<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2047016448820670464/_nOVnpOm_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456/regent0x%5F)<br/>[regent0x](http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456/regent0x%5F)<br/>[@regent0x\_](http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456/regent0x%5F)<br/>·<br/>16h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow regent0x\_<br/>3<br/>4<br/>43<br/>[3K](http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456/regent0x%5F/status/2062099361199263928/analytics)<br/>anthropic shipped something last week that most people completely missed<br/>while you're sitting there waiting for one claude session to grind through a task, claude code can now fan that same task out across 100 agents working in parallel - all from a single prompt<br/>it's called dynamic workflows. and the framing from anthropic is blunt: work you'd normally plan in quarters now finishes in days<br/>this isn't hype. someone used it to rewrite 750,000 lines of code in 11 days. i'll break down exactly how that happened, when to use it, how to not bankrupt yourself on tokens, and the exact prompts to copy<br/>save this one. it's long because it's a real playbook, not a teaser<br/>## <br/>.what dynamic workflows actually are<br/>first, let's clear up the confusion. claude code now has three different ways to run multiple agents, and people mix them up constantly. picking the wrong one wastes money and time<br/>→ subagents \- run inside a single session, do one well-defined task, report back to the parent. consistent and cost-predictable. they can't talk to each other<br/>→ agent teams \- multiple full claude sessions that coordinate, message each other, and divide work. powerful, but every message between agents is a billable round trip<br/>→ dynamic workflows \- the new one. claude writes its own orchestration scripts, breaks your task into subtasks, and fans them out across tens to hundreds of parallel subagents in a single session<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ4IvlAXUAI0HIC?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456/regent0x%5F/article/2062099361199263928/media/2062095292862779394)<br/>the key difference with dynamic workflows: you don't design the orchestration. claude does<br/>you give it a big task. it plans the fan-out, runs the agents, has other agents try to refute what the first ones found, iterates until the answers converge, and hands you one coordinated result<br/>one engineer at CyberAgent described it as the gap-filler \- it sits between firing off a single subagent and hand-building a full agent team. you describe the problem, claude figures out how to parallelize it, and the plan stays on track no matter how big the task gets because the coordination happens outside the conversation<br/>## <br/>.the proof - 750,000 lines in 11 days<br/>the example that made people pay attention: the rewrite of Bun<br/>Jarred Sumner used dynamic workflows to port Bun from Zig to Rust. the numbers are absurd:<br/>* roughly 750,000 lines of Rust<br/>* 99.8% of the existing test suite passing<br/>* 11 days from first commit to merge<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ4JKjZWwAAQrk2?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2062094052170899456/regent0x%5F/article/2062099361199263928/media/2062095756287197184)<br/>here's how it actually worked, because the process is the entire lesson:<br/>→ workflow 1 mapped</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 51 · 🔁 4 · 💬 3 · 🔖 87</p><p><a href="https://x.com/regent0x_/status/2062099361199263928">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
