## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ0xMgiX0AAyEi-?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096/tricalt/article/2061860170846843233/media/2061858295367847936) How sandboxes + shared memory could unlock the next big discovery...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Vasilije</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/tricalt">@tricalt</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096">http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096">http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ0xMgiX0AAyEi-?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096/tricalt/article/2061860170846843233/media/2061858295367847936)<br/>How sandboxes + shared memory could unlock the next big discovery<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1456192254515814403/cHVJ7L7g_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096/tricalt)<br/>[Vasilije](http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096/tricalt)<br/>[@tricalt](http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096/tricalt)<br/>·<br/>Jun 1<br/>·<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow tricalt<br/>2<br/>18<br/>104<br/>[7.8K](http://x.com/i/article/2057167874033156096/tricalt/status/2061860170846843233/analytics)<br/>How many breakthroughs came from carrying a solution out of one field into another?<br/>A lot, but let’s check some that are relevant to AI only:<br/>* Diffusion models: thermodynamics → image generation.<br/>* Black–Scholes: the heat equation → options pricing.<br/>* Convolutional nets: the visual cortex → computer vision.<br/>The same solution pattern transfers from completely different domains and enriches the new fields. You can now have knowledge that lived in one field, reach another, and make something new become possible.<br/>## <br/>Sandboxes + agents in the same domain<br/>Sandboxes were the agent story of the year, and they matter more every month as agents get more independent. Each agent gets its own isolated computer to run code and experiment without breaking something that matters.<br/>The problem shows up the moment you run more than one. Every sandbox starts cold. Cold start is when an agent works out how your system behaves every time it starts.. The next agent re-derives the same thing. So does the one after that. You pay for the same discovery again and again. It's a tax on every multi-sandbox workflow. We saw this firsthand at our last hackathon with <br/>[@daytonaio](https://x.com/@daytonaio)<br/>, led by <br/>[@ivanburazin](https://x.com/@ivanburazin)<br/> , one of the sharpest teams in the sandbox space. <br/>And “reuse knowledge” here isn't the whole story. Usually these agents run at the same time, so you want the learnings shared live, the moment one of them finds something.<br/>Concrete example: You point a research agent at problem X and attack from three directions at once: three agents, three sandboxes, in parallel. A, B, and C.<br/>Agent A grinds through a hard sub-problem. Many tokens, a couple of hours, and it cracks it. Later B hits the same wall, but its prompt was framed slightly differently and it stalls where A didn't. Without shared memory, B burns more time and compute, and what A learned never leaves A's sandbox. With shared memory, A's result is written the moment it lands. B checks memory, skips the wall, keeps moving. No agent re-pays for a discovery another already made.<br/>## <br/>Sandboxes + agents in different domains (The real unlock!)<br/>Now that we went through the efficiency case, let’s elaborate on the most interesting one: sharing knowledge across different domains.<br/>Look at the examples again:<br/>* Diffusion models: thermodynamics → image generation.<br/>* Black–Scholes: the heat equation → options pricing.<br/>* Convolutional nets: the visual cortex → computer vision.<br/>* Velcro: burdock burrs → fasteners.<br/>* Sonar: bat echolocation → submarine detection.<br/>None of these pairs share similar terminologies. A burr is not a fastener, the heat equation is not an option. What they share is a shape. Someone recognized that a problem in one field had the same structure as a solved problem in another, and carried the solution across. If you could reliably abstract problems and solutions into patterns and map them between domains, you'd solve a huge share of the problems that look hard only because no one has connected them yet.<br/>The catch has always been that this mapping lived in one head. The transfer only happened if the burrs and the fas</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 173 · 🔁 28 · 💬 3 · 🔖 307</p><p><a href="https://x.com/tricalt/status/2061860170846843233">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
