## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ0NXqeWMAAB05A?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912/Mnilax/article/2061850337716187144/media/2061818904595279872) The 2-agent setup that cut my Claude Code frontend rework from 39% to 6% (Exact Routing Inside)...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Mnimiy</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/Mnilax">@Mnilax</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912">http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912">http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ0NXqeWMAAB05A?format=jpg&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912/Mnilax/article/2061850337716187144/media/2061818904595279872)<br/>The 2-agent setup that cut my Claude Code frontend rework from 39% to 6% (Exact Routing Inside)<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2007608177492217856/3gdItGwC_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912/Mnilax)<br/>[Mnimiy](http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912/Mnilax)<br/>[@Mnilax](http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912/Mnilax)<br/>·<br/>9h<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow Mnilax<br/>6<br/>3<br/>43<br/>[2.9K](http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912/Mnilax/status/2061850337716187144/analytics)<br/>The setup is one routing file and one rule: Claude Code keeps the logic, a frontend-only specialist agent called Kombai takes everything from props to pixels. Across 40 tasks that took first-try compile from 58% to 95%, time-to-merge on UI down 44%, and duplicate components from 27 to 2\. The exact ROUTING.md is at the end.<br/>Same specialist. I just stopped asking one agent to do the one job it's worst at.<br/>A coding agent ships backend like a staff engineer and frontend like it's never opened your design system. That's not a model weakness. It's a job-description problem: one generalist hired to be a systems architect and a UI craftsman in the same session, paid top-of-stack reasoning prices for work that's 70% repeatable grunt.<br/>Below: where the generalist breaks on frontend, the routing split that fixes it, the numbers across 40 tasks, and a copy-paste ROUTING.md you can drop in today. The file's at the end if you want to skip the explanations.<br/># <br/>Why this matters<br/>Frontend teams spend 60 to 70 percent of their time on repeatable grunt: wiring components, matching tokens, fixing responsive edge cases, re-implementing patterns that already live three folders over. That's the figure Kombai's founders use to frame the problem they're attacking, and it matches what anyone shipping UI feels by Thursday.<br/>Here's the trap. That 70% is the cheap work. The remaining 30%, hierarchy, interaction, what a screen is even for, is the expensive judgment you're actually paid for. Point a generalist at all of it and you invert the cost: you burn the most expensive reasoning tokens in your stack on the cheapest work, and still get a guess on the part that needed you.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ0Nk10XoAI9Rab?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912/Mnilax/article/2061850337716187144/media/2061819130978738178)<br/>A generalist isn't bad at frontend because it's weak. It's bad at your frontend because it has no idea what your frontend is. It hasn't read your component library. It can't see your rendered DOM. Backend hides this. A function returns the right shape or it doesn't. UI fails in a thousand soft ways that all compile clean.<br/># <br/>The 4 ways a generalist breaks on frontend<br/>Each of these came out of the 40-task run. I'll show the moment, then the cost.<br/>## <br/>Break 1: It guesses your design system<br/>The generalist has no token map, so it reaches for defaults that are sensible everywhere except your codebase. Spacing close-enough but off your scale. Font weight one step wrong. A shadow that exists nowhere else in the app.<br/>The moment: Asked for a pricing card. Got padding: 16px against a 4px scale where every other card uses a spacing.4 token resolving to 20px. Eight cards on the page. Seven were tokenized, one was hardcoded, and it took a designer flagging it in review to catch the 4px gap. Individually trivial. Collectively the difference between "our product" and "a demo."<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ0RWKHXwAAiQKv?format=png&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2061818708226342912/Mnilax/article/2061850337716187144/media/2061823276775620608)<br/>## <br/>Break 2: It rebuilds wha</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 67 · 🔁 7 · 💬 6 · 🔖 98</p><p><a href="https://x.com/Mnilax/status/2061850337716187144">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
