## To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark [View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts) [![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ0-IJ_W4AA8Fe-?format=png&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384/sydneyrunkle/article/2061873579004735884/media/2061872514247090176) Introducing Rubrics: Build Agents that Evaluate and Correct Their Work...</description>
      <content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><strong>Sydney Runkle</strong> (<a href="https://x.com/sydneyrunkle">@sydneyrunkle</a>)</p><blockquote><p><a href="http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384">http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384</a></p></blockquote><hr/><p>🔗 <a href="http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384">http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384</a></p><blockquote>## <br/>To view keyboard shortcuts, press question mark<br/>[View keyboard shortcuts](http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384/i/keyboard%5Fshortcuts)<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ0-IJ_W4AA8Fe-?format=png&amp;name=900x900)](http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384/sydneyrunkle/article/2061873579004735884/media/2061872514247090176)<br/>Introducing Rubrics: Build Agents that Evaluate and Correct Their Work<br/>[![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/1955622250629021696/WOrOq2Mw_normal.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384/sydneyrunkle)<br/>[Sydney Runkle![](https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/2028336270431453184/jRRpLAdG_bigger.jpg)](http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384/sydneyrunkle)<br/>[@sydneyrunkle](http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384/sydneyrunkle)<br/>·<br/>7h<br/>·<br/>Follow<br/>Click to Follow sydneyrunkle<br/>4<br/>18<br/>103<br/>[24K](http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384/sydneyrunkle/status/2061873579004735884/analytics)<br/>Authored by <br/>[@sseshadri43](https://x.com/@sseshadri43)<br/> and <br/>[@sydneyrunkle](https://x.com/@sydneyrunkle)<br/>## <br/>TL;DR<br/>* Agents often produce outputs that head in the right direction but don't fully land on the first attempt.<br/>* RubricMiddleware is how you tell the agent what "done" looks like — and make it keep going until it gets there.<br/>* Most effective for tasks with clear, verifiable success criteria like passing tests, avoiding forbidden patterns, covering required sections.<br/>Agents are taking on more complex tasks than ever. More often than not, they fall short of the finish line. We've added RubricMiddleware to Deep Agents to fix that — you define a rubric, and the agent self-evaluates and iterates until it satisfies every criterion, or hits a configured cap.<br/>If you're familiar with /goal in Claude Code or Codex, this is a similar pattern. This implementation is a bit more flexible because evaluation is handled by a dedicated grader sub-agent that can call tools, reason over the full transcript, and return per-criterion feedback.<br/># <br/>The problem<br/>Some agent tasks have a clear definition of "done." A code refactor is finished when the test suite passes. A report is complete when every required section is covered.<br/>But agents don't always get there on the first try. As context grows larger, ambiguous instructions, tool misuse, and non-deterministic errors all compound — output quality deteriorates, and developers are left to diagnose and re-run tasks manually.<br/># <br/>How it works<br/>Before the agent run finishes, a separate grader sub-agent reviews it against the rubric. If everything passes, the run concludes. If anything falls short, the grader's per-criterion feedback is injected back into the conversation and the agent runs again. The loop terminates when the rubric is satisfied, or when a configured iteration limit is hit.<br/>[![Image](https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HJ09cXHWQAI3G3Q?format=jpg&amp;name=small)](http://x.com/i/article/2061868304654864384/sydneyrunkle/article/2061873579004735884/media/2061871761856020482)<br/>The loop terminates on \`satisfied\`, \`max\_iterations\_reached\`, \`failed\`, or \`grader\_error\`.<br/>## <br/>Wiring it up<br/>Here's the minimal setup, broken into steps. The key idea: define a RubricMiddleware once, attach it to a deep agent, then pass a rubric string at invoke time. (If rubric is absent, the middleware does nothing.)<br/>## <br/>1) Define RubricMiddleware<br/>This middleware adds a grader loop on top of the base agent. The grader is configured with:<br/>* model: the LLM used for grading (often smaller/cheaper than your main agent model)<br/>* system\_prompt: instructions that define the grader’s role and what “good” looks like<br/>* tools: optional tools the grader can call to gather evidence (e.g., run tests, lint, validate outputs)<br/>* max\_iterations: the maximum number of fix → re-grade loops before the run stops<br/>python<br/>```python<br/>from deepagents import RubricMiddleware<br/>rubric_middleware = RubricMiddleware(<br/>    model="anthropic:claude-haiku-4-5",<br/>    system_prompt="You are a code revie</blockquote><p style="color:#777;font-size:0.9em">♥ 193 · 🔁 27 · 💬 5 · 🔖 358</p><p><a href="https://x.com/sydneyrunkle/status/2061873579004735884">View on X →</a></p>]]></content:encoded>
    </item>
